{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underpricing (horizon 10–30d): when the recent 5–10d slope turns positive after a drawdown, the model lags and stays too low; apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small in week 1, larger by weeks 3–4).",
    "Spike/step-change lag: after a sharp upside break (e.g., +2 to +4 in 1–3 days), the model partially follows but remains below the new regime for ~1–2 weeks; add a catch-up term proportional to the size of the break, decaying over ~10 trading days.",
    "Over-mean-reversion in late horizon: beyond ~day 15 the model often pulls forecasts back toward ~63–66 even when realized prices persist in the high-60s; reduce mean-reversion strength when the last 20–30d range shifts upward (higher highs/higher lows).",
    "Down-move handling is better than up-move handling: early-horizon errors during declines are small, but during rebounds the model underestimates; use asymmetric correction—stronger positive adjustments than negative ones for the same recent momentum magnitude.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too smooth vs. realized outcomes (miss higher peaks); widen forecast distribution/allow higher amplitudes when recent realized volatility increases (e.g., 10d stdev rising)."
  ]
}
